(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-lipids"]={"title":"Lipid-Lowering Drugs Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statin has MINIMAL CYP metabolism, so CYP3A4 inhibitors affect it least?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin's major metabolic route is listed as minimal CYP, unlike the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statin is a CYP3A4 SUBSTRATE, raised by verapamil and grapefruit juice?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Lovastatin","Pravastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are the CYP3A4 statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statin is metabolized mainly by CYP2C9?","choices":["Simvastatin","Atorvastatin","Fluvastatin","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluvastatin's major metabolic enzyme is CYP2C9, not CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statin is cleared by GLUCURONIDATION (UGT enzymes) rather than CYP?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin","Fluvastatin","Pitavastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pitavastatin's major metabolic enzymes are UGT1A3 and UGT2B7. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statin can be used as HIGH-INTENSITY therapy?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Pravastatin","Fluvastatin","Lovastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only atorvastatin and rosuvastatin reach high intensity, lowering LDL (low-density lipoprotein) by 50% or more. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which is the OTHER statin, besides atorvastatin, that reaches HIGH INTENSITY?","choices":["Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pitavastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only two high-intensity statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which drug is the CHOLESTEROL ABSORPTION INHIBITOR, acting at the intestinal brush border?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe selectively inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption, raising hepatic LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor expression. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug's concentrations are LOWERED BY ANTACIDS?","choices":["Rosuvastatin","Fenofibrate","Alirocumab","Ezetimibe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antacids and bile acid sequestrants decrease ezetimibe concentrations; cyclosporine increases them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug ACTIVATES PPAR-ALPHA to lower triglycerides and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Atorvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha: more fatty acid oxidation, less VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein), more ApoA-1 and HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which drug INCREASES the ANTICOAGULANT EFFECT of warfarin?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which fibrate is named as a RELATIVE CONTRAINDICATION with statins?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Bezafibrate","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gemfibrozil sits on the statin relative contraindication list with cyclosporine, niacin and erythromycin, because of myopathy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is a BILE ACID SEQUESTRANT?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Fenofibrate","Pravastatin","Cholestyramine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The bile acid sequestrants are cholestyramine, colestipol and colesevelam. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED when TRIGLYCERIDES exceed 400 mg/dL?","choices":["Colestipol","Fenofibrate","Gemfibrozil","Niacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resins may raise VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides; above 400 mg/dL is an absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering drug is APPROVED IN PREGNANCY because it is not absorbed?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Cholestyramine","Fenofibrate","Simvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed, so they are approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drug INTERFERES WITH ABSORPTION of digoxin, warfarin and thyroxine?","choices":["Evolocumab","Fenofibrate","Colesevelam","Rosuvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anion-exchange resins bind these drugs in the gut; give them 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug causes PROSTAGLANDIN-MEDIATED FLUSHING, minimized by aspirin?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Niacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is prostaglandin mediated; aspirin premedication minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which drug RAISES URIC ACID and glucose, so gout and diabetes are relative contraindications?","choices":["Niacin","Gemfibrozil","Ezetimibe","Cholestyramine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At larger doses niacin raises liver enzymes, glucose and uric acid; chronic liver disease is its absolute contraindication. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT EFFECTIVE as an antilipemic?","choices":["Nicotinic acid","Niacinamide","Extended-release niacin","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form; its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which drug produces the LARGEST RISE IN HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Niacin","Pravastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nicotinic acid raises HDL by 15 to 35%, the largest rise of any class. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which drug causes CHOLELITHIASIS and is contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease?","choices":["Niacin","Colestipol","Rosuvastatin","Fenofibrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates cause cholelithiasis and are contraindicated in existing gallbladder disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which drug is an injectable MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY against PCSK9?","choices":["Alirocumab","Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are the injectable-only, expensive PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug's MOST SERIOUS adverse reaction is HYPERSENSITIVITY?","choices":["Simvastatin","Evolocumab","Niacin","Fenofibrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity reactions are the most serious adverse reaction to PCSK9 inhibitors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which drug and its active GLUCURONIDE metabolite circulate ENTEROHEPATICALLY?","choices":["Cholestyramine","Atorvastatin","Ezetimibe","Evolocumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enterohepatic recirculation returns ezetimibe to its site of action and limits systemic exposure. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug is FIRST LINE when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering drugs are indicated?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Gemfibrozil","Atorvastatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Statins are the most efficacious and best tolerated agents and are first line for LDL lowering. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
